{"qid":"Q7038","names":["County of Barcelona"],"title":"County of Barcelona","endonym":{"native":"Girona","lang":"ca"},"capsule":"The County of Barcelona was established in 801 when Frankish forces under Louis the Pious captured Barcelona from the Umayyads, installing Count Berà as the first Carolingian appointee. Under Wilfred the Hairy (died 897), the county became hereditary, and his successors progressively absorbed neighboring Catalan counties while conducting persistent warfare against the Caliphate of Córdoba. Count Borrell II's refusal to renew his oath to the Capetian king Hugh Capet after Almanzor's sack of Barcelona in 985 marked de facto independence. Ramon Berenguer IV's 1137 betrothal to Petronilla of Aragon joined the county to the Kingdom of Aragon; their son Alfonso II, ruling from 1162, became the first monarch to hold both titles simultaneously, ending the county's separate identity.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The county's boundaries are reasonably reconstructible from Carolingian and papal documents, though the precise limits of its southward Reconquista expansion at any given decade remain approximate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: County of Barcelona","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/County_of_Barcelona"},{"label":"Britannica: Barcelona (county)","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Barcelona-county"}],"britannica":"place/Girona-Spain","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Girona","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Bera","nativeName":"Berà","roman":"Bera","start":801,"end":820,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Rampon","nativeName":